      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I haven't noticed any color banding, but I did notice some of my images have extreme dithering on them, and it turns out those are the ones I had auto-focus enabled on. \nBelow, you can see two photos cropped and resized 300% with nearest neighbor in Photoshop. On the left, the autofocus engaged, and the image is dithered and messy looking. On the right, it did not, and as a result the image is clean and you can actually make out the black paisley pattern on my black hoodie.\nIn short, dithering isn't improving photos, it's making them markedly worse. Perhaps its necessary to avoid banding when you have depth of field. I can't exactly test for that. But with no depth of field, the image without the dithering looks much more detailed.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-11-06T12:18:50.064Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "According to release notes of build 1144, it seems that dithering is added for cameras. However, it will not work for pictures without the focusing feature because the dithering implementation is combined with the DoF simulation.\nIn my opinion, color banding issues are not related to the DoF simulation and the dithering is useful for deeply focused pictures too. Could you enable dither regardless of whether focusing or not?",
